    "maintainer_can_modify": true,
    "title": "net: Use exclusive P2P listen sockets on Windows",
    "user": {
      "login": "winterrdog",
      "id": 87463258,
      "node_id": "MDQ6VXNlcjg3NDYzMjU4",
      "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/87463258?v=4",
      "gravatar_id": "",
      "url": "https://api.github.com/users/winterrdog",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/winterrdog",
      "followers_url": "https://api.github.com/users/winterrdog/followers",
      "following_url": "https://api.github.com/users/winterrdog/following%7B/other_user%7D",
      "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/users/winterrdog/gists%7B/gist_id%7D",
      "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/winterrdog/starred%7B/owner%7D%7B/repo%7D",
      "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/winterrdog/subscriptions",
      "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/winterrdog/orgs",
      "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/winterrdog/repos",
      "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/winterrdog/events%7B/privacy%7D",
      "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/winterrdog/received_events",
      "type": "User",
      "site_admin": false,
      "name": null,
      "patch_url": null
    },
    "body": "on Windows, `SO_REUSEADDR` does not carry over the same semantics as on\r\nPOSIX systems. a second socket can set `SO_REUSEADDR` and bind to a\r\nport that is _already in use_ by another process running under\r\nthe _same_ user account. this means another local process could bind to\r\nthe P2P listen port and receive incoming connections intended for\r\n`bitcoind`.\r\n\r\nthis matters because the P2P listener is expected to have exclusive\r\nownership of its address and port. allowing another process to bind to\r\nit could let that process _intercept_ incoming P2P connections. this is\r\nin a similar spirit to #36169, which made the RPC listener exclusive on\r\nWindows.\r\n\r\nto fix this, we use `SO_EXCLUSIVEADDRUSE` for the P2P listen socket on\r\nWindows instead of `SO_REUSEADDR`, while retaining `SO_REUSEADDR` on\r\nother platforms. the added test verifies the expected behaviour by\r\nattempting to bind a second socket to the same port. on Windows, this\r\nfails with `WSAEACCES` (`10013`), which Python exposes as\r\n`PermissionError` (an `OSError` subclass).\r\n\r\nreferences:\r\n\r\n- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/winsock/using-so-reuseaddr-and-so-exclusiveaddruse\r\n- https://stackoverflow.com/questions/14388706/how-do-so-reuseaddr-and-so-reuseport-differ/14388707#14388707",
